[{:seon.task/name "a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.cost/unit-benches` :db/pull-attr measures pull [:note/text :note/agent] minus pull [:note/text] on one entity n times, and `seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current` compares it with the table row (11.6 KB per attribute today). Reproduction (33f6283, one binary): 11.6 KB on one run and 8.9 KB on the next two; bisecting over 9 revisions gave either value, and the Jolt pin is not the cause (the same on 00a3b321 and 9e42a0d8). Cause: the unit depends on Datahike's caches (:search-cache-size, the store cache) being warm or cold at its start, so the row is a coin flip and the test flakes. Correct: a unit measures the work alone under a stated cache (a fresh db per measurement, or a declared warm-up that settles it); the two modes are gone over 10 runs; then the row is re-measured on the release binary (bin/check units)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current seon.cost-test/a-unit-measures-the-same-across-runs}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-page-says-regression"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a cost report carries its verdict and rows but not its estimate's recorded overrun, so `seon.cost/verdict-lines` has no estimate there and the page's verdict box cannot say REGRESSION. Correct: the verdict carries the regression (`seon.cost/judge`), and both faces (the text and the page) read it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/a-regression-is-in-the-verdict}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proof-measures-only-its-own-thread"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): `seon.profile/alloc-bytes` reads the calling thread's own allocation (Chez branch jolt/thread-bytes keeps thread_gc.mutator_bytes on the mutator's allocation paths, read as (cs)thread_bytes_allocated), and `seon.profile/cpu-ns` (CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID) is a proof's time, so a cost proof is immune to the rest of the process. Before, alloc-bytes was Chez's process-wide (sstats-bytes (statistics)): 1,712 B over 200 ms idle, 1,426,698,112 B over the same window while another thread allocated, and `seon.program-cost-test/recording-a-form-is-flat-in-the-program` went red once inside a full reach (16x bytes, tree ac2743c) and green alone. Measured after: one call's least-of-25 reads 74,448 B alone and beside an allocating thread, where the process-wide count read 74,608 alone and 170,384 beside (2.3x, red against the proof's 1.25x). Not taken: recording the process's other allocation and answering :unmeasured (honest, but it cannot recover the true number)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-speedup-never-reads-as-steeper"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (worktree session store, under seon.program-cost-test/a-change-re-folds-only-what-reaches-it): seon.cost/judge fits a dimension's growth on measured less the estimate's :fixed. When a change makes the work cheaper, the fixed part falls under the stated :fixed, and the smallest size's remainder shrinks toward zero, so the fitted exponent jumps. Jolt 0d39edc3 took reach-update from 500k to 427k ticks at 100 and from 1.24M to 1.07M at 1,000, under the estimate at both, and the fit on (427k - 420k, 1.07M - 420k) read n^1.96: red for a speedup. Re-deriving each proof's :fixed after every speedup is churn. Correct, in judge: growth is judged so that a uniform speedup can never read as steeper. Done (branch jolt/a-speedup-never-reads-as-steeper, docs/cost.md#how-growth-is-judged): with three sizes or more, each size's increment over the smallest is fitted against the work's own (no :fixed); with two, one increment has no shape, so the :fixed taken off is bounded by what the smallest size leaves beyond its estimated per-unit part; constant work's cost itself is fitted. Bounding :fixed alone (tried first) let a second pass whose per-unit estimate is loose at small n read as linear, so three sizes use the increments. Proof: a seon.cost-test case whose rows are another case's minus a constant (a speedup of the fixed part) gives the same steeper verdict, and the reach-update rows above are not steeper."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/a-speedup-never-reads-as-steeper}}
 {:seon.task/name "cost-tolerances-are-derived"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.cost/slack` {:ns 3.0 :bytes 1.25 :calls 1.0} (bytes called exact, yet 25% because the unit table flakes, task seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache: 11.6 vs 8.9 KB; ns 3x for load), `seon.cost/steeper-by` 0.3 and `seon.cost/ratchet` 1.5 are guesses; test/seon/profile_test.clj asserts within 2x and only when contracts are on (zero assertions when off). Correct: each unit row records its spread over k runs under a declared cache state; a dimension's slack is the spread of the units its estimate prices (bytes then near 1.0); ns carries the run's load and its spread at that load; the exponent tolerance is derived per dimension and proof as log(slack_d)/log(n_max/n_min) (over 100x: bytes 1.25 -> 0.048, ns 3 -> 0.24); the ratchet is the overrun's own measured spread at filing; every verdict prints the derived tolerances, and the agent's prompt renders them from seon.cost; profile_test asserts the sampling rule exactly (ceil(n/64) timed calls) with contracts on. Tried: jolt/unitflake (2838e62..cc43b5b; retargeted diff next/notes/archive/worktrees/unitflake-retargeted-2026-10-22.diff, applies on 5af7b4b); not merged because on main it turns three green cost proofs red, so the derivation is wrong: the calls exponent tolerance derives to 0.000 (every unit row's calls range is exactly 1.0) and a fixed per-call overhead bends a linear fit (`seon.docs-cost-test/an-unchanged-reload-costs-a-parse-and-a-pull-per-section` n^1.01, `seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass` n^1.03), and the ratchet tightened to 1.26x puts `seon.program-cost-test/recording-a-form-is-flat-in-the-program` past its open overrun. The plan owes: an exponent tolerance that holds for exact dimensions (from the fit's residual, or the estimate's fixed term), the ratchet's rule stated, then the unit table re-measured with seon.cost-test/table (:pr-str/char measures 33.05 B against the row's 29.36)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/the-exponent-tolerance-follows-slack-and-span
                      seon.cost-test/slack-is-the-units-measured-spread
                      seon.context-test/the-prompt-states-the-judges-own-tolerances}}
 {:seon.task/name "ticks-are-exact"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (worktree session store) under seon.source-test/a-fixed-change-costs-the-same-at-any-program-size: the transact row's ticks differed between runs, 406.8k / 425k / 479.6k at 10 / 100 / 1,000 in one bin/prove shard against 406.3k / 424.8k / 477.1k in another process, 0.5% at 1,000, enough to move a growth fit by 0.02. docs/cost.md calls ticks exact. Reproduction (scratch script, seon.profile/measure! of d/db-with on seon.source-test/transact-of 10, 40 samples per process): within a process the count is exact, 406,355-406,362 per sample. The first call reads 409,285 (warming, which measure's warm call takes). About one sample in 40 reads 1,000 more (Chez's timer quantum, docs/cost.md). Holding 300 MB of live data first changes nothing (no collection ran during any sample). Two causes. (1) Fixed: seon.cost/measure repeated a call until 20 ms of CPU time or 25 calls, a clock deciding how many samples a counted dimension's minimum is taken over: a fast call took 25 and a slow or loaded one 3. It now takes seon.cost/repeats (3) after its warm call, whatever a call costs (seon.cost-test/a-measure-takes-a-count-of-samples-never-a-time: 26 calls before, 4 after). (2) Not yet found: the shard's process counts 0.1-0.5% more ticks for the same call than a fresh one. It is not the heap, and not a collection. It is what ran earlier in that process, e.g. protocol-dispatch caches (Jolt's PIC and resolve-memo, invalidated by every registration), Datahike's global caches, or the store's id counters giving other entity ids and so other index paths. Next: measure the row in one fresh process after requiring and running each other test namespace of its shard, bisect to the one that moves the count, and fix at that state's owner, so the same call counts the same in any process."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/a-measure-takes-a-count-of-samples-never-a-time}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-cost-proof-is-the-same-under-load"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: Found 2026-10-23 by the cutover's decider: red in a built scratch worktree of main 4bc598dc1 as well as in the fresh clone, so it is main's, not the cutover's. In a loaded 4-shard `bin/test --all` (load average about 12) `seon.vector-cost-test/a-search-reads-each-row-once` (bytes grew as n^1.96, linear allows n^1.88; per-n ratios 1.04, 1.02, 1.00) and `seon.program-cost-test/a-probed-entity-is-sought-not-scanned` (ticks n^0.62, log allows n^0.60; ratios 0.89-0.94) went red; run alone, each is green in the same tree and in main. AGENTS section 5 says ticks and bytes are exact and the same on any machine, so a number that moves with load is measuring something else (bytes allocated by a concurrent thread? a GC in the window?). Find what moves and exclude it from the count, or widen the fit's tolerance on the exponent with the measured spread (cost-tolerances-are-derived). Replaces nothing. Cause of the bytes red (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-24, main 9347fae1): Chez's per-thread count (S_thread_bytes_allocated, jolt/thread-bytes d19eb3bf) charges a collection's own bytes to the thread it lands on. In one thread of plain Jolt with a live heap, the count across an explicit (collect) is +17,344 B the first time and +512 B after that; an empty window is 0. Under seon.cost/measure, seon.vector/near reads 5,794,752 B at 500 and 6,655,536 B at 2,000 quietly, and 67,264 B more at both sizes beside a second allocating thread, which puts a collection in every measured call; its ticks do not move. One such offset on the 500 size and not the 1,000 one moves the increments fit from a ratio of 3 to 3.89, n^1.96 against the 1.88 allowed: the red. In 300 samples of a fixed call beside an allocating thread, 297 are exact; the rest are high by a 16 KB segment or so, each with a collection or collect request in its window. Next: in c/alloc.c and gcwrapper.c, find what a collection adds to mutator_bytes outside the during_alloc gate (the post-collection allocation-area reset, or the collector's own conses) and exclude it there; a Chez test that bytes across (collect) are 0."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.vector-cost-test/a-search-reads-each-row-once seon.program-cost-test/a-probed-entity-is-sought-not-scanned}}]
